---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro'
import EnvTable from '../components/EnvTable.astro'
import Step from '../components/Step.astro'
import { parseEnvExample, readRepoFile } from '../lib/repo'

const compose = readRepoFile('docker/compose.yaml')
const keycloakCompose = readRepoFile('docker/keycloak/compose.yaml')
const entries = parseEnvExample(readRepoFile('docker/.env.example'))
const keycloakEntries = parseEnvExample(readRepoFile('docker/keycloak/.env.example'))
---
<Page
  title="Install PSPad"
  description="Run PSPad on your own hardware with Docker Compose: the stack, every environment variable, the secrets, and the first sign-in."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Install</h1>
    <p>
      Two Compose stacks joined by one Docker network, <code>psplace</code>.
      <strong>Keycloak</strong> (<code>docker/keycloak/compose.yaml</code>) runs Keycloak
      and its Postgres database. Its accounts serve every self-hosted app on the
      <strong>psplace</strong> realm, not only PSPad. <strong>PSPad</strong>
      (<code>docker/compose.yaml</code>) runs the API, the client and MongoDB. Each
      stack can back itself up every night. Budget a few minutes for the first build.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Docker with Compose v2 (<code>docker compose version</code> prints v2.x).</li>
      <li>About 2 GB of disk.</li>
      <li>Beyond <code>localhost</code>: a reverse proxy for TLS. The stack serves plain HTTP only.</li>
    </ul>

    <Step n={1} title="Clone the repository">
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/psp515/PSPad.git
cd PSPad</code></pre>
    </Step>

    <Step n={2} title="Create both environment files">
      <pre><code>cp docker/keycloak/.env.example docker/keycloak/.env
cp docker/.env.example docker/.env</code></pre>
      <p>
        Do this first, or the stacks start with empty values. Defaults work on
        <code>localhost</code>, except <code>KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD</code>, which you
        must set: the Keycloak stack refuses to start without it. Change
        <code>MONGO_PASSWORD</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> before either
        stack is reachable from anywhere else. Keep <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code>
        and <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> the same in both files.
      </p>
      <p><code>docker/keycloak/.env</code>:</p>
      <EnvTable entries={keycloakEntries} />
      <p><code>docker/.env</code>:</p>
      <EnvTable entries={entries} />
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Two Keycloak URLs">
        <p>
          <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> is for your <strong>browser</strong>.
          <code>KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_AUTHORITY</code> is for the <strong>API container</strong> —
          they reach Keycloak by different names, so <code>localhost</code> means something
          different to each. Running an external Keycloak? Set both to the same address.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <Callout kind="warning" title="The admin credentials apply once to Keycloak, but the API keeps reading them">
        <p>
          In <code>docker/keycloak/.env</code>, <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code> and
          <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> only bootstrap Keycloak on its very first
          start. If you change the password in the Keycloak console afterwards, editing
          that file does nothing to Keycloak. The <code>api</code> container reads its own copy
          in <code>docker/.env</code> at runtime, every time it calls Keycloak's admin API
          to delete a user's login on account deletion. If you rotate the password in the
          console without also updating <code>docker/.env</code> and restarting
          <code>api</code>, account deletion keeps wiping the user's data but silently
          stops removing their Keycloak login.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <Step n={3} title="Start Keycloak, then PSPad">
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml up -d
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
      <p>
        Keycloak goes first because its stack creates the <code>psplace</code> network
        the API joins. The PSPad stack fails with <em>network psplace not found</em>
        until it exists.
      </p>
      <p>The first build takes a few minutes. Mongo starts as a replica set — required for transactions. Wait for it to report healthy:</p>
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml ps</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>api</code> and <code>app</code> (and <code>keycloak</code> in its own stack) show a bare
        <code>Up</code> forever — that's normal. Confirm the API is actually answering:
      </p>
      <pre><code>curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{'{'}http_code{'}'}\n" http://localhost:5000/api/today</code></pre>
      <p><code>401</code> is correct — you're just not signed in yet. Anything else means the stack didn't come up cleanly.</p>
      <Callout title="The API runs in Production mode, on purpose">
        <p>
          It can still reach Keycloak over plain HTTP because <code>compose.yaml</code> sets
          <code>Keycloak__RequireHttpsMetadata: "false"</code> for the <code>api</code> service
          directly — not because it's in Development mode. Putting Keycloak behind HTTPS later?
          Flip that value instead of setting <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code>, which would
          also leak stack traces to anyone who can reach the API.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <Step n={4} title="Create your first user">
      <p>No registration screen — accounts live in Keycloak, and you need one before the app is usable.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open the admin console at <code>KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME</code>
          (<code>http://localhost:8080</code> by default) and sign in with
          <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code> / <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>.
        </li>
        <li>Switch realm from <strong>master</strong> to <strong>psplace</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Users</strong> → <strong>Create new user</strong>. Fill in username, email,
          first and last name.
        </li>
        <li>
          Then <strong>Credentials</strong> → <strong>Set password</strong>, with
          <em>Temporary</em> off — otherwise Keycloak forces a password change the client
          can't show.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </Step>

    <Step n={5} title="Sign in">
      <p>
        Open <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>, sign in, and land on My Day — empty at first,
        while three starter areas provision in the background. Install it from your
        browser's address bar for the offline app.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Give it 20–30 seconds before you trust something is saved">
        <p>
          The app is offline-first: new tasks save to your browser immediately and sync to
          the server every 20–30 seconds. Restart the stack right after creating something
          and it's gone — it never made it to MongoDB.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <Callout title="Sessions are kept long on purpose">
        <p>
          The bundled realm keeps sessions for <strong>30 days idle, 90 days maximum</strong> —
          long enough that an installed PWA stays signed in for as long as the app's own
          offline trust window allows. Realm <strong>psplace</strong> is shared across every
          self-hosted application it serves, so this lifetime applies to all of them, not
          only PSPad.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <h2>The stacks, in full</h2>
    <p><code>docker/keycloak/compose.yaml</code>, exactly as it ships:</p>
    <pre><code>{keycloakCompose}</code></pre>
    <p><code>docker/compose.yaml</code>, exactly as it ships:</p>
    <pre><code>{compose}</code></pre>

    <h2>Putting it behind a proxy</h2>
    <p>
      Point three hostnames at the published ports — client <code>5001</code>, API
      <code>5000</code>, Keycloak <code>8080</code> — terminate TLS at the proxy, and forward
      <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Set <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>, <code>API_BASE_ADDRESS</code> and
        <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> in <code>docker/.env</code>, and
        <code>KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME</code>, <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code> and
        <code>API_BASE_ADDRESS</code> in <code>docker/keycloak/.env</code>, to your public
        <code>https://</code> URLs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Update the Keycloak client too, or sign-in fails.</strong> The bundled realm
        ships with <code>http://localhost:5001/*</code> baked into its redirect URI — changing
        <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code> doesn't touch it. Realm <strong>psplace</strong> →
        <strong>Clients</strong> → <strong>pspad-frontend</strong> → update
        <strong>Valid redirect URIs</strong>. <strong>Web origins</strong> follows
        automatically (it's <code>+</code>, unless you've changed it).
      </li>
      <li>
        Set <code>API_BEHIND_PROXY=true</code> in <code>docker/.env</code>. Public snapshot
        links are rate-limited per visitor address; without it every visitor arrives from the
        proxy's address and they all share one limit. Only do this once port <code>5000</code>
        is no longer reachable except through the proxy — otherwise a caller can forge
        <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>.
      </li>
      <li>Recreate both stacks:
        <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml up -d --force-recreate
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --force-recreate</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>MongoDB and Postgres publish no port. Each is reachable only from its own stack.</p>

    <h2>Backups</h2>
    <p>Everything worth keeping lives in two volumes, one per stack:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-data</code>: areas, lists, tasks and the event log.</li>
      <li>
        <code>keycloak_keycloak-db-data</code>: accounts and realm settings.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <code>pspad_mongo-keyfile</code> only holds the replica-set key. A restore
      doesn't need it, because a fresh install generates its own.
    </p>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Never pass -v to docker compose down">
      <p>
        <code>down -v</code> on the Keycloak stack deletes every account. PSPad keys its
        users by the Keycloak user's UUID, so accounts recreated afterwards are new people.
        The old areas, lists and tasks stay in MongoDB, owned by a user who can no longer
        sign in. On the PSPad stack, <code>down -v</code> deletes the data and the local
        backups with it. <code>down</code> on its own is safe.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Turn on nightly backups</h3>
    <p>
      Each stack has a backup service behind the <code>backup</code> profile:
      <code>mongo-backup</code> beside MongoDB, and <code>keycloak-backup</code> beside
      Keycloak's Postgres. Both are the same small .NET worker, built from
      <code>src/PSPad.Backup</code>. Start them with their stacks:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml --profile backup up -d
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml --profile backup up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>Every night at <code>BACKUP_AT</code> each service:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Dumps its database: a point-in-time <code>mongodump --oplog</code> for PSPad, a
        <code>pg_dump</code> for Keycloak.
      </li>
      <li>Keeps the dump in its <code>*-backups</code> volume, or in <code>BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR</code> if you set one.</li>
      <li>If you configured S3 or Google Drive, encrypts a copy with your age key and uploads it.</li>
      <li>Deletes copies older than <code>BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS</code>, but never after a failed run.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Keycloak's default time is half an hour after PSPad's. Restoring a pair from the
      same night then never gives you accounts older than the data that belongs to them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run a backup now, or list what you have:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml run --rm mongo-backup now
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml run --rm mongo-backup list</code></pre>
    <p>
      Each service turns <em>unhealthy</em> in <code>docker compose ps</code> when its
      last successful run is more than 26 hours old. To be told rather than having to
      look, set <code>BACKUP_PING_URL</code> to a healthchecks.io-style URL. It is called
      after every run, with <code>/fail</code> appended when the run failed.
    </p>

    <h3>Off-host copies, always encrypted</h3>
    <p>
      Create an <a href="https://age-encryption.org">age</a> key pair on a machine
      other than the server:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker run --rm --entrypoint age-keygen ghcr.io/psp515/pspad-backup-mongo:latest &gt; pspad-backup.key</code></pre>
    <p>
      Put the <code>age1…</code> public key from that file into
      <code>BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT</code> in both <code>.env</code> files. Store the file
      itself somewhere safe, away from the server. Without it, the off-host copies can't
      be read by anyone, you included. A backup service with S3 or Google Drive set and no
      recipient refuses to start.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>S3-compatible</strong> (AWS, Backblaze B2, Cloudflare R2, MinIO): set
        <code>BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT</code>, <code>BACKUP_S3_BUCKET</code> and the key pair.
        Both stacks can share one bucket: each writes below
        <code>&lt;prefix&gt;/mongo/</code> or <code>&lt;prefix&gt;/keycloak/</code>.
        Turn on versioning or object lock, so a leaked key can't erase your history.
        If the key may not delete, set <code>BACKUP_REMOTE_PRUNE=false</code> and let a
        bucket lifecycle rule handle retention.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Drive</strong>: Drive uploads need your own Google OAuth client.
        <ol>
          <li>
            In the Google Cloud console, create a project, enable the <em>Google Drive
            API</em>, and create an OAuth client of type <em>TVs and Limited Input
            devices</em>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Put its id and secret into <code>BACKUP_GDRIVE_CLIENT_ID</code> and
            <code>BACKUP_GDRIVE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Sign in once:
            <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml run --rm mongo-backup gdrive-login</code></pre>
            It prints a URL and a code. Open the URL on any device, enter the code, and
            approve. It then prints <code>BACKUP_GDRIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN=…</code>. Copy that line
            into both <code>.env</code> files.
          </li>
        </ol>
        The backups only get the <code>drive.file</code> scope. They can only see files
        they created themselves: a <code>pspad-backups</code> folder in your My Drive, with
        one subfolder per stack. Publish the client's consent screen: a client left in
        <em>Testing</em> issues tokens that expire after 7 days.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Restore</h3>
    <p>
      Stop the service that writes to the database, restore, and start it again. The
      restore refuses to run while that service still accepts connections.
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml stop keycloak
docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml run --rm keycloak-backup restore latest
docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml start keycloak

docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml stop api
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml run --rm mongo-backup restore latest
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml start api</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>latest</code> takes the newest local copy. To pick another one, pass a name
        from <code>list</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--from s3</code> or <code>--from gdrive</code> fetches the copy from
        off-host instead. This needs the private key: set
        <code>BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY_FILE</code> to its path for the duration of the restore.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each restore asks you to type the database name. <code>--yes</code> skips that.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Restore Keycloak, then MongoDB. Pair a MongoDB backup with the first Keycloak backup
      taken at or after it. Restoring <code>latest</code> on both stacks from the same
      night does exactly that.
    </p>
    <Callout title="Every device re-syncs after a MongoDB restore">
      <p>
        A restore gives the database a new identity. Each signed-in device notices on its
        next sync, drops its local copy and pulls everything again. Changes a device
        hadn't sent yet are still sent afterwards. Any that refer to something the restore
        took back show up as rejected in Settings → Sync. Anything written after the
        backup was taken is gone.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      A MongoDB restore leaves Mongo's own users alone, so <code>MONGO_PASSWORD</code>
      stays whatever it is now. Without the backup profile, you can still dump the
      database by hand:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml exec -T mongo \
  sh -c 'mongodump --archive --username pspad --password "$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD" --authenticationDatabase admin' \
  &gt; pspad-backup.archive</code></pre>
    <p>
      Keep the single quotes — the password only exists inside the <code>mongo</code>
      container. Double quotes would expand it on your host instead, where it's empty.
    </p>

    <h2>Upgrading, restarting</h2>
    <pre><code>git pull
docker compose -f docker/keycloak/compose.yaml up -d
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      Volumes survive — only <code>down -v</code> destroys them. Give Mongo 10–30 seconds to
      report healthy again before assuming something's broken.
    </p>

    <h2>Two things worth knowing</h2>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Keycloak runs in development mode">
      <p>
        Its data lives in Postgres now, which makes it safe to back up and restore. But
        <code>start-dev</code> relaxes Keycloak's own hardening, so treat it as a
        single-household setup. Take a backup before a major image upgrade.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout title="Coming from the single-stack layout?">
      <p>
        Keycloak used to run inside <code>docker/compose.yaml</code> on an embedded H2
        database, in the <code>pspad_keycloak-data</code> volume. The new Keycloak stack
        starts fresh from the bundled realm and doesn't read that volume. Its accounts are
        not carried over, and the volume can be removed once you no longer need it.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="The old keyfile is in the git history">
      <p>
        Early commits included <code>docker/mongo-keyfile</code>. Yours generates its own on
        first start and never uses that one. The old key only matters to someone who can
        already reach your MongoDB, which publishes no port.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Page>
